WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.760
You are testing a new feature, you scroll through a list, tap an element, and the interface

2
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:11.120
suddenly tears or freezes mid-action. When this happens in a React Native application,

3
00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:16.640
the standard diagnostic reflex is to open the React DevTools profiler to investigate component

4
00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:21.920
render times. The resulting flame chart provides a detailed breakdown of the JavaScript thread.

5
00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:27.280
It identifies exactly which components re-rendered, the specific state changes that triggered them,

6
00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:32.960
and the exact millisecond duration of the JavaScript execution. But you will inevitably encounter a

7
00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:37.760
specific anomaly. The DevTools profiler will show that your components rendered in less

8
00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:42.480
been a single millisecond, yet the device in your hand continues to visibly drop frames.

9
00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:47.680
This discrepancy confirms that the JavaScript logic is executing efficiently. The bottom

10
00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:53.360
neck is not in your component code. It exists further down the rendering pipeline. In the new architecture,

11
00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:58.240
data must cross structural boundaries. If the delay is missing from the JavaScript thread,

12
00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:03.760
the stall is occurring during the C++ layout engine calculations, or during the final native

13
00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:09.920
view mutations. Relying solely on React DevTools for performance profiling in the new architecture

14
00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:16.080
creates a diagnostic blind spot. Finding the root cause requires moving beyond the JavaScript thread

15
00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:21.600
to observe the complete execution timeline. This conceptual graphic displays the React Native

16
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:27.280
architecture as three distinct layers, the JavaScript thread, the C++ and JS hybrid,

17
00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:32.640
and the native thread. To diagnose cross thread failures, engineers rely on a tool called

18
00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:38.160
Perfetto. While Hermes and React DevTools profile the top layer of JavaScript execution,

19
00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:44.160
Perfetto captures absolute OS level metrics across all boundaries simultaneously. It transforms

20
00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:49.520
performance optimization from a process of elimination into a strict mathematical packing problem.

21
00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:56.240
By deconstructing a Perfetto trace, we can see the exact microsecond where JavaScript IO,

22
00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:02.720
C++ layout engines, and native rendering operations compete for the exact same block of time.

23
00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:09.120
The health of a system built on the new architecture is defined by the application's ability to fit

24
00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:14.080
all of these cross boundary operations into a single, inflexible time window.

25
00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:20.560
This is a simplified representation of the Perfetto timeline. To locate a performance bottleneck,

26
00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:26.880
we start with a blank horizontal track. The most critical marker on this timeline is a slice called

27
00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:33.760
choreographer due frame. This marker establishes the Android V-sync frame budget, a strict

28
00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:40.080
interval dictated by the physical hardware of the device's screen. At a standard 60 frames per second

29
00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:47.600
refresh rate, the application has exactly 16.6 milliseconds to complete all processing for a single frame.

30
00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:54.640
This 16.6 millisecond boundary is the definitive standard against which Perfetto measures all success

31
00:02:54.640 --> 00:03:00.320
or failure in your rendering pipeline. Performance optimization relies on strictly packing every

32
00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:06.160
required calculation and mutation into this tight hardware constraint. To measure our exact budget

33
00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:12.080
utilization, we examine the fabric commit trace slice. This block tracks the complete lifecycle

34
00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:17.760
of the new C++ rendering pipeline. A healthy fabric commit operation needs to complete in under

35
00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:23.200
8 milliseconds. Because the commit finishes well inside the V-sync deadline, the screen updates

36
00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:28.400
incredibly smoothly on the user's device. But if the commit pipeline is overloaded with complex

37
00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:34.080
calculations, that block expands. When the fabric commit extends past the red line, the hardware

38
00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:39.840
deadline is missed. The device drops the frame, creating visible UI stutter. Identifying the dropped

39
00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:45.840
frame is the starting point. Using Perfetto, we subdivide the overflowing fabric commit block to

40
00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:52.000
isolate specific sub-operations causing the delay. The fabric commit is not a single action.

41
00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:57.440
It is a container for several distinct C++ and JSI operations executing sequentially.

42
00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:03.600
The first sub-metric inside the block is the yoga layout pass. If this specific calculation takes

43
00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:08.320
longer than two milliseconds, it indicates that the shadow tree is processing too many nodes.

44
00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:14.320
This bloat is typically caused by excessively deep view hierarchies and large measure in window calls

45
00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:19.920
executing during the layout calculation. The second sub-metric is the JSI host function trace.

46
00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:25.360
This tracks the exact duration of each host function call, which is where the JavaScript thread

47
00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:30.800
interacts with synchronous turbo modules. If a poorly implemented turbo module performs expensive

48
00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:36.560
synchronous IO operations, it stalls the JavaScript thread. This inflates the host function slice

49
00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:42.400
and pushes the parent commit block past the deadline. The final sub-metric is the mounting transaction

50
00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:49.200
execution. This tracks the exact time required to apply the calculated C++ mutations down to the

51
00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:55.840
native Android or iOS views. By isolating these three specific slices, you transition from guessing

52
00:04:55.840 --> 00:05:01.600
why a UI is slow to having targeted proof of the bottleneck's location. With the exact bottleneck

53
00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:07.440
isolated in the trace, we can implement precise code-level optimizations. We begin by addressing the

54
00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:13.520
bloated yoga layout slice. Relying on the default collapsible true property, flatens the node hierarchy

55
00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:18.240
in the shadow tree, immediately reducing the layout calculation time to under two milliseconds.

56
00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:24.480
Next, we address the JSI slice by refactoring the synchronous turbo module call to execute

57
00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:30.480
asynchronous IO instead. Moving the IO off the JavaScript thread removes the blocking behavior,

58
00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:36.400
rapidly shrinking the host function duration. As these sub-metric shrink, the overall fabric commit block

59
00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:43.760
retracts. True performance optimization in the new architecture is visually confirmed when all C++

60
00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:51.520
and JSI blocks are safely packed inside that 16.6 millisecond window. React dev tools remains highly

61
00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:57.040
effective for identifying inefficient component renders and redundant state updates in the JavaScript

62
00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:03.040
layer. But Perfetto is the only tool that accurately visualizes the synchronous realities and

63
00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:08.480
cross-thread dependencies of the new architecture. Cross-referencing the choreographer frame deadline

64
00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:13.920
dense the fabric commit block provides the empirical data required to verify that the rendering

65
00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:19.680
pipeline meets the hardware's timing requirements. Analyzing these traces allows for the precise

66
00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:29.840
isolation of bottlenecks that standard JavaScript profilers cannot detect.

